Background and setup. Consider disordered statistical systems whose effective low-energy behavior is dominated by strong, frozen-in randomness. Two complementary renormalization-group (RG) philosophies have been used to study such systems:

(a) Strong-disorder RG (SDRG): one iteratively decimates the strongest local couplings/fields, tracking only the growing tails (rare events) of certain disorder distributions. This approach has been carried out successfully for a small number of special models (e.g. random transverse-field Ising chains, certain random spin chains, random walks/diffusion in random media, and a few related problems), where the flow drives the system to an "infinite-disorder" fixed point characterized by exponentially broad distributions.

(b) Functional renormalization group (FRG): one writes a flow equation for an entire functional (typically a renormalized disorder correlator R(u) or, more generally, the joint probability distribution P[\,\cdot\,] of a disorder field), keeping the full functional dependence rather than only a few moments.

Problem. Construct a single functional renormalization-group framework that simultaneously:

1. Applies to strong-disorder systems whose fixed points are of infinite-disorder type (so that typical and rare events are governed by very different scales);
2. Flows the full probability distribution P_\ell of the relevant disorder degree(s) of freedom under coarse-graining at scale \ell, rather than only the rapidly diverging moments or the extreme tails associated with rare events, so that statistics of typical events are captured;
3. Reduces, in appropriate limits, to the established SDRG decimation rules on the known solvable strong-disorder models (such as random transverse-field Ising chains, random Heisenberg/XX chains, Sinai diffusion, and related cases);
4. Extends beyond this short list of solved examples, providing controlled flow equations and computable fixed-point distributions for a broader class of strong-disorder problems (for instance higher-dimensional random quantum magnets, random elastic manifolds in the strong-pinning regime, or generic systems with broadly distributed couplings).

Question. Does such a unified FRG framework for strong-disorder systems exist, and if so, exhibit it explicitly: give the flow equation acting on the full distribution, identify its infinite-disorder fixed points, and demonstrate consistency with the known SDRG results on the solvable benchmark models while producing new predictions for at least one model outside that list.
